@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Woven Document

@s One Source, Two Woven Formats

The HTML and LaTeX paths will both use this small document:

````text
@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s
@title Distance

@s Squared Distance
The formula $d^2 = x^2 + y^2$ gives the squared distance.
The implementation is in @{distance.rs}.

--- distance.rs
fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    x * x + y * y
}
---
````

Parser records one semantic section containing a prose occurrence and a code
occurrence. Prose recognizes the sentence as text, an inline equation, more
text, and a named-block reference. Resolver decides that the reference and
the code definition name the same block. Those are source facts, but neither
HTML nor LaTeX syntax has been chosen yet.

Litweb next prepares the meaning that every woven output must preserve:

```text
presentation section 1, "Squared Distance"
|-- paragraph
|   |-- equation                 d^2 = x^2 + y^2
|   `-- reference                distance.rs -> section 1
`-- root code definition         distance.rs
```

A **backend** is one supported kind of woven output, such as HTML or LaTeX. A
**renderer** is the part of a backend that writes its particular syntax. The
**prepared woven document** is the source content together with shared
presentation sections, named-block relationships, and identifier locations.
It is a useful name for these cooperating values, not one additional parsed
file or public Rust type.

Both renderers receive the facts above. HTML can represent the equation with
a marked `span` and the reference with a link to `#1:1`. LaTeX can write the
equation as `\(d^2 = x^2 + y^2\)` and link the same visible section number
to a safe anchor such as `litweb-1-1`. The surrounding syntax differs; the
section division, equation source, block identity, and destination do not.

An **output context** is the exact place where a renderer is about to put
authored text: a title, ordinary prose, code, a link target, or mathematics,
for example. Each renderer later escapes text for that context. Context belongs
to the output format, while the prepared meaning belongs to both.

@s The Shared Module

This private module owns the common locations and relationships. HTML and
LaTeX import them instead of deriving separate answers.

--- src/woven.rs
//! Presentation sections and relationships shared by output backends.

use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};
use std::path::PathBuf;

use crate::identifier::{IdentifierIndex, IdentifierKind, IdentifierRole};
use crate::parser::{BlockKind, Modifier, Program, SourceOrigin};
use crate::resolver::{BlockLookup, ResolvedProgram};
use crate::util::block_reference;

@{Presentation section locations}
@{Presentation sections}
@{Weave layout model}
@{Weave layout implementation}
@{Woven relationship index model}
@{Resolve book chapter links}
@{Located identifier index}
@{Located mini-index meanings}
@{Collect weave locations}
---

@s Three Views of the Source

Preparing a woven document combines three views that answer different
questions:

```text
Program
|-- authored prose and code occurrences
`-- their physical source locations

ResolvedProgram
|-- one stable identity for each named definition
|-- final name lookup
`-- output roots

IdentifierIndex
|-- spellings at parser coordinates for a final index
`-- declaration meanings and their associated occurrences
```

The presentation layout comes from `Program`, because both renderers display
authored occurrences. Named-block relationships combine that layout with
`ResolvedProgram`, because a base, its changes, and its uses must meet at one
definition identity. Target-language links combine the layout with
`IdentifierIndex`.

Prose has already grouped source lines into paragraphs, lists, fences, tables,
and equations and has recognized their inline elements. Preparation keeps that
content and adds reader-facing locations; it does not parse the prose a second
time.

@s A Larger Relationship Example

The one-section `Distance` document established the shared boundary. A richer
example shows why preparation must finish before either renderer starts
writing. Resolver and Tangler followed this program as its changes turned
`Greeting line` into the final contents of `hello.txt`:

```text
@title A Greeting
@s Greeting
This small program writes one line.

--- hello.txt
@{Greeting line}
---

The text comes from a separate named piece.

--- Greeting line
Hello
---

--- Greeting line +=
from Litweb
---

--- Greeting line :=
Good morning
---

--- Greeting line +=
from Litweb
---
```

Tangler needs only the final definition: `Good morning` followed by
`from Litweb`. A woven document instead keeps every authored occurrence so a
reader can see how the program was assembled. The one semantic section
beginning at `@s Greeting` becomes five numbered presentation sections:

```text
1  "Greeting"
   prose and the hello.txt root, which uses Greeting line
2  prose and the base Greeting line definition
3  the first addition
4  the replacement
5  the final addition
```

The root in section 1 refers to `⟨Greeting line 2⟩`. The relationship facts
say that sections 3 and 5 add to the base, section 4 replaces it, and section
1 uses it. Since those links can point forward, preparation derives every
location before an HTML or LaTeX renderer writes its first section.

@s One Location System for Section Links

The layout and both indexes share one location system. That agreement is what
lets a heading, a named-block relationship, and an identifier entry all point
to the same numbered section.

@s Deriving the Five Reading Sections

Parser creates one semantic `Section` when it sees `@s Greeting`. That section
controls command scope and owns every prose and code occurrence in the
example. It is not yet the five-part outline a reader sees.

The layout starts with an empty presentation section. The opening prose fills
its prose slot, and the `hello.txt` root fills its code slot, giving section 1.
The next prose cannot follow an occupied code slot, so it begins section 2;
the base `Greeting line` block shares that section. Each later code
occurrence finds the current code slot occupied and therefore starts sections
3, 4, and 5.

That walk gives the general rules:

- prose followed by code shares one presentation section;
- code followed by prose produces two;
- prose followed by two code blocks produces two;
- a `noWeave` code occurrence occupies no visible slot;
- a blank prose block occupies no prose slot; and
- an explicitly empty `@s` still produces one empty presentation section.

Only section 1 carries the semantic title `Greeting`. The other four are
untitled continuations, not new command scopes. Their presentation numbers
are stable reading locations; they are neither physical source-line numbers
nor Parser's semantic-section number.

@s Naming a Reading Location

`SectionLocation` records three facts: which chapter owns a location, the
chapter number printed for readers, and the chapter-local presentation
number. In the one-file example, the base block's location produces anchor
`1:2` and local label `2`. In a book, the same value can also produce a
cross-chapter label such as `2.4`.

`WeaveLayout.chapters` stores the five derived sections.
`block_locations` is a parallel lookup table shaped like the parsed
`chapter → semantic section → block occurrence` hierarchy. The root occurrence
maps to section 1, the base to section 2, and the three changes to sections 3,
4, and 5. Blank prose and `noWeave` code map to `None`.

Every later index asks this one table where an occurrence became visible.
Named-block links and identifier links therefore cannot invent different
split points.

--- Presentation section locations
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub(crate) struct SectionLocation {
    pub chapter: usize,
    pub chapter_number: String,
    pub section: usize,
}

impl SectionLocation {
    pub fn anchor(&self) -> String {
        format!("{}:{}", self.chapter_number, self.section)
    }

    pub fn label(&self, current_chapter: Option<usize>) -> String {
        if Some(self.chapter) == current_chapter {
            self.section.to_string()
        } else {
            format!("{}.{}", self.chapter_number, self.section)
        }
    }
}
---

@s What One Presentation Section Records

A `WeaveSection` points back to the semantic section that owns it and selects
at most one prose occurrence and one code occurrence. For example, section 2
selects the second prose occurrence and the base code occurrence from the
single `Greeting` section. Its location contains presentation number 2.

`source_section_start` is false for that continuation. It is true only for
section 1, which is why `Greeting` appears once instead of above every change.

--- Presentation sections

#[derive(Debug)]
pub(crate) struct WeaveSection {
    pub source_section: usize,
    pub source_section_start: bool,
    pub prose: Option<usize>,
    pub code: Option<usize>,
    pub location: SectionLocation,
}

impl WeaveSection {
    fn new(
        chapter: usize,
        chapter_number: &str,
        source_section: usize,
        source_section_start: bool,
        number: usize,
    ) -> Self {
        Self {
            source_section,
            source_section_start,
            prose: None,
            code: None,
            location: SectionLocation {
                chapter,
                chapter_number: chapter_number.to_owned(),
                section: number,
            },
        }
    }
}
---

@s The Complete Layout

The complete layout owns both representations: reader-facing presentation
sections and the occurrence-to-location lookup table. Keeping them together
protects their agreement.

--- Weave layout model

#[derive(Debug)]
pub(crate) struct WeaveLayout {
    pub chapters: Vec<Vec<WeaveSection>>,
    block_locations: Vec<Vec<Vec<Option<SectionLocation>>>>,
}
---

@s The Two Layout Operations

The layout has two operations. Construction derives all presentation
sections; lookup answers where one parsed occurrence became visible.

--- Weave layout implementation
impl WeaveLayout {
    @{Derive the weave layout}
    @{Find a parsed block location}
}
---

@s Walking the Parsed Occurrences

Construction walks every parsed occurrence once. `current` is the
presentation section being filled. When the next visible occurrence cannot
fit, the algorithm saves `current` and starts the next numbered section.

The location assigned to an occurrence is cloned into `block_locations` at
the same moment, so the two representations cannot drift apart. Every
semantic section contributes at least its initial presentation section, even
when an explicit `@s` is otherwise empty.

--- Derive the weave layout

pub fn new(program: &Program) -> Self {
    let mut chapters = Vec::with_capacity(program.chapters.len());
    let mut block_locations = Vec::with_capacity(program.chapters.len());

    for (chapter_index, chapter) in program.chapters.iter().enumerate() {
        let mut sections = Vec::new();
        let mut chapter_locations = Vec::with_capacity(chapter.sections.len());
        let chapter_number = chapter.number();

        for (source_section_index, source_section) in
            chapter.sections.iter().enumerate()
        {
            let mut locations = vec![None; source_section.blocks.len()];
            let mut current = WeaveSection::new(
                chapter_index,
                &chapter_number,
                source_section_index,
                true,
                sections.len() + 1,
            );

            for (block_index, block) in source_section.blocks.iter().enumerate() {
                match &block.kind {
                    BlockKind::Prose => {
                        if !block
                            .lines
                            .iter()
                            .any(|line| !line.text.trim().is_empty())
                        {
                            continue;
                        }
                        if current.prose.is_some() || current.code.is_some() {
                            sections.push(current);
                            current = WeaveSection::new(
                                chapter_index,
                                &chapter_number,
                                source_section_index,
                                false,
                                sections.len() + 1,
                            );
                        }
                        current.prose = Some(block_index);
                        locations[block_index] = Some(current.location.clone());
                    }
                    BlockKind::Code(code) => {
                        if code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::NoWeave) {
                            continue;
                        }
                        if current.code.is_some() {
                            sections.push(current);
                            current = WeaveSection::new(
                                chapter_index,
                                &chapter_number,
                                source_section_index,
                                false,
                                sections.len() + 1,
                            );
                        }
                        current.code = Some(block_index);
                        locations[block_index] = Some(current.location.clone());
                    }
                }
            }

            sections.push(current);
            chapter_locations.push(locations);
        }

        chapters.push(sections);
        block_locations.push(chapter_locations);
    }

    Self {
        chapters,
        block_locations,
    }
}
---

@s Looking Up One Occurrence

Later indexing and rendering provide Parser's chapter, semantic-section, and
block coordinates. The parallel table returns the one presentation location
assigned during construction. A blank prose or `noWeave` occurrence returns
`None` because it has no visible target.

--- Find a parsed block location

pub fn block_location(
    &self,
    chapter: usize,
    source_section: usize,
    block: usize,
) -> Option<&SectionLocation> {
    self.block_locations[chapter][source_section][block].as_ref()
}
---

@s Indexing Definitions, Changes, and Uses

Resolver assigns the base `Greeting line` definition and all three changes
one stable identity, `blocks[1]`. The shared relationship entry for that
identity is exact:

```text
definition       section 2
additions        sections 3 and 5
replacement      section 4
uses             section 1
```

The entry uses separate fields because the page gives each relationship
different wording. In Rust, replacements retain the older field name
`redefinitions`.

Several authored occurrences contribute to this one entry. Each first obtains
its presentation location from `WeaveLayout`; Resolver's lookup then selects
the stable definition identity. Repeated relationships in one presentation
section collapse without changing source order.

A hidden base definition records `definition_hidden` instead of inventing an
HTML target. Visible prose or code references then remain unlinked `⟨Name⟩`
text. Whole-line uses are recognized by the same helper Tangler uses, so
displayed and expanded references obey one grammar.

--- Woven relationship index model
#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub(crate) struct BlockLocations {
    pub definition: Option<SectionLocation>,
    pub definition_hidden: bool,
    pub additions: Vec<SectionLocation>,
    pub redefinitions: Vec<SectionLocation>,
    pub uses: Vec<SectionLocation>,
}

pub(crate) struct WeaveIndex {
    pub blocks: HashMap<usize, BlockLocations>,
    pub roots: HashSet<usize>,
    chapter_paths: Option<Vec<PathBuf>>,
}
---

@s Resolving an Authored Chapter Link

Suppose `routes/start.lit` contains `Read [The map](../maps/map.lit).`
The containing chapter contributes directory `routes`; the parent component
removes it, leaving declared source path `maps/map.lit`. That path identifies
one chapter. HTML will use its page, while LaTeX will use its heading inside
the combined book. Neither renderer needs to open another source file.

The relationship index retains the manifest-relative paths in chapter order.
An absent path list means standalone input, where ordinary links keep their
authored targets. In a book, no requesting chapter means introduction prose
in the manifest directory. Lookup returns either an ordinary link, a chapter
position, or an invalid chapter reference. The renderer pairs a failure with
the inline link's physical source origin.

Only plain relative `.lit` targets opt into this lookup. URL schemes,
absolute paths, queries, and fragments keep their ordinary link meaning.
Within a candidate, `.` and `..` are resolved without filesystem access;
walking above the manifest directory fails. A literal percent sign remains
part of the filename, not an instruction to decode a URL escape.

--- Resolve book chapter links
impl WeaveIndex {
    pub fn chapter_link(
        &self,
        target: &str,
        requesting_chapter: Option<usize>,
    ) -> Result<Option<usize>, ()> {
        let Some(paths) = &self.chapter_paths else {
            return Ok(None);
        };
        if !target.ends_with(".lit")
            || target.starts_with('/')
            || target.contains([':', '?', '#', '\\'])
        {
            return Ok(None);
        }
        let mut path = requesting_chapter
            .and_then(|chapter| paths[chapter].parent())
            .unwrap_or_else(|| std::path::Path::new(""))
            .to_path_buf();
        for component in target.split('/') {
            match component {
                "" => return Err(()),
                "." => {}
                ".." => {
                    if !path.pop() {
                        return Err(());
                    }
                }
                name => path.push(name),
            }
        }
        paths
            .iter()
            .position(|candidate| *candidate == path)
            .map(Some)
            .ok_or(())
    }
}
---

@s Target-Language Identifier Locations

The named-block relationship index answers questions about `Greeting line`.
The target-language identifier index answers separate questions about names
such as the Rust type `Graph`.

Identifier ended with this exact `Notes` section:

````text
@code_type rust .rs
@s Notes
Call @code{parse_str()} before continuing.

```text
@s Not a section
--- Not a block
@code{not_rust()}
```

Name | Example
--- | ---
type | @code{Graph}

--- graph.rs
struct Graph {
    vertex_count: usize,
}

fn make_graph(initial_count: usize) -> Graph {
    let graph = Graph {
        vertex_count: initial_count,
    };
    graph
}
---
````

Identifier reports four `Graph` occurrences at Parser coordinates: the prose
use, the struct definition, the return-type use, and the struct-expression
use.

The layout puts the prose occurrence and following `graph.rs` block in one
presentation section. All four source occurrences therefore map through the
layout to the same location. The first use creates that location, the
definition upgrades its role, and the remaining uses do not duplicate or
downgrade it. The displayed identifier index contains one underlined
definition link for `Graph`. This spelling-oriented result is suitable for the
final index, which asks where a name occurs throughout the document.

--- Located identifier index
#[derive(Default)]
pub(crate) struct LocatedIdentifierIndex {
    pub entries: Vec<LocatedIdentifierEntry>,
}

pub(crate) struct LocatedIdentifierEntry {
    pub name: String,
    pub locations: Vec<LocatedIdentifierLocation>,
}

pub(crate) struct LocatedIdentifierLocation {
    pub location: SectionLocation,
    pub role: IdentifierRole,
}

pub(crate) fn locate_identifiers(
    source: &IdentifierIndex,
    layout: &WeaveLayout,
) -> LocatedIdentifierIndex {
    let mut entries = Vec::new();
    for entry in source.entries() {
        let mut locations: Vec<LocatedIdentifierLocation> = Vec::new();
        for occurrence in &entry.occurrences {
            let Some(location) = layout.block_location(
                occurrence.site.chapter,
                occurrence.site.section,
                occurrence.site.block,
            ) else {
                continue;
            };
            if let Some(existing) = locations
                .iter_mut()
                .find(|existing| existing.location == *location)
            {
                if occurrence.role == IdentifierRole::Definition {
                    existing.role = IdentifierRole::Definition;
                }
            } else {
                locations.push(LocatedIdentifierLocation {
                    location: location.clone(),
                    role: occurrence.role,
                });
            }
        }
        if !locations.is_empty() {
            entries.push(LocatedIdentifierEntry {
                name: entry.name.clone(),
                locations,
            });
        }
    }
    LocatedIdentifierIndex { entries }
}
---

@s Preparing Meanings for Physical Pages

The mini-index follows the definition-oriented view. Continue Identifier's
example: section 4 on an earlier spread defines `struct Graph`; the current
left and right pages use `Graph` as a return type and parameter type. The
desired reminder is `Graph: struct, 4.` If section 4 moves onto either current
page, that reminder must disappear.

Woven Document cannot make that decision yet. A **presentation section** is a
logical reading location, not a physical page. A short section may share a
page with several neighbors, while a long code section may cross a page
boundary. Font metrics and the mini-index itself can also move material to the
next page. Assigning every occurrence in section 12 to one predicted page
would therefore be wrong.

Instead, this stage prepares exact notes for the LaTeX renderer. Each meaning
receives a small positive **page marker**, an internal number that identifies
the meaning. Marker 1 might stand for the complete printable entry
`Graph: struct, 4.` The number is not a section number and will not be visible
in the PDF. The definition already has its linked presentation location.

Each associated definition or use retains its @code{SourceOrigin} and original
column. Together they identify the precise token that should carry the
invisible marker, even when one source line contains several identifiers.
Meanings are sorted before marker numbers are assigned, so the result is
deterministic. Occurrences on hidden presentation blocks are omitted because
no renderer can put a marker beside invisible text.

The resulting @code{LocatedMiniIndex} still contains no page numbers. It says
what each marker means and where every marker belongs in visible source. LaTeX
will insert the notes, and its page composer will decide which of them
actually land on the same spread. HTML uses the spelling-oriented locations
above and needs no mini-index markers.

--- Located mini-index meanings
#[derive(Default)]
pub(crate) struct LocatedMiniIndex {
    pub meanings: Vec<LocatedMiniMeaning>,
    pub occurrences: HashMap<SourceOrigin, Vec<LocatedMiniOccurrence>>,
}

pub(crate) struct LocatedMiniMeaning {
    pub marker: usize,
    pub name: String,
    pub kind: IdentifierKind,
    pub definition: SectionLocation,
}

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub(crate) struct LocatedMiniOccurrence {
    pub marker: usize,
    pub role: IdentifierRole,
    pub column: usize,
}

pub(crate) fn locate_mini_identifiers(
    source: &IdentifierIndex,
    layout: &WeaveLayout,
) -> LocatedMiniIndex {
    let mut selected = source
        .meanings()
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|meaning| {
            layout
                .block_location(
                    meaning.definition.chapter,
                    meaning.definition.section,
                    meaning.definition.block,
                )
                .cloned()
                .map(|definition| (meaning, definition))
        })
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();
    selected.sort_by(|(left, left_location), (right, right_location)| {
        left.name
            .to_lowercase()
            .cmp(&right.name.to_lowercase())
            .then_with(|| left.name.cmp(&right.name))
            .then_with(|| left.kind.cmp(&right.kind))
            .then_with(|| left_location.chapter.cmp(&right_location.chapter))
            .then_with(|| left_location.section.cmp(&right_location.section))
            .then_with(|| left.id.cmp(&right.id))
    });

    let mut source_to_marker = vec![None; source.meanings().len()];
    let meanings = selected
        .into_iter()
        .enumerate()
        .map(|(position, (meaning, definition))| {
            let marker = position + 1;
            source_to_marker[meaning.id] = Some(marker);
            LocatedMiniMeaning {
                marker,
                name: meaning.name.clone(),
                kind: meaning.kind,
                definition,
            }
        })
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();

    let mut occurrences: HashMap<SourceOrigin, Vec<LocatedMiniOccurrence>> =
        HashMap::new();
    for occurrence in source.meaning_occurrences() {
        let Some(marker) = source_to_marker[occurrence.meaning] else {
            continue;
        };
        if layout
            .block_location(
                occurrence.site.chapter,
                occurrence.site.section,
                occurrence.site.block,
            )
            .is_none()
        {
            continue;
        }
        occurrences
            .entry(occurrence.site.origin.clone())
            .or_default()
            .push(LocatedMiniOccurrence {
                marker,
                role: occurrence.role,
                column: occurrence.column,
            });
    }
    for located in occurrences.values_mut() {
        located.sort_by_key(|occurrence| (occurrence.column, occurrence.marker));
    }

    LocatedMiniIndex {
        meanings,
        occurrences,
    }
}
---

@s Collecting Relationship Locations

Collection begins with one empty entry for every resolved definition and a
set of the identities that are output roots. It then walks authored code
occurrences in source order. One step records whether the occurrence is a
base, addition, or replacement; the other scans its lines for uses. A small
helper keeps each location list unique without disturbing its first-seen
order.

For books, collection also retains the declared chapter source paths in that
same chapter order. These paths are the complete lookup namespace for authored
chapter links, even when a chapter has no presentation sections or identifiers.

--- Collect weave locations
pub(crate) fn collect_locations(
    program: &Program,
    resolved: &ResolvedProgram,
    layout: &WeaveLayout,
) -> WeaveIndex {
    let mut blocks = (0..resolved.blocks.len())
        .map(|block| (block, BlockLocations::default()))
        .collect::<HashMap<_, _>>();
    let roots = resolved.roots.iter().map(|root| root.block).collect();

    for (chapter_index, chapter) in program.chapters.iter().enumerate() {
        for (section_index, section) in chapter.sections.iter().enumerate() {
            for (block_index, block) in section.blocks.iter().enumerate() {
                @{Index a code occurrence}
                @{Index whole-line uses}
            }
        }
    }

    let chapter_paths = program.book().map(|_| {
        program
            .chapters
            .iter()
            .map(|chapter| {
                chapter
                    .book
                    .as_ref()
                    .expect("book chapter")
                    .source_path
                    .clone()
            })
            .collect()
    });
    WeaveIndex {
        blocks,
        roots,
        chapter_paths,
    }
}

fn push_unique(locations: &mut Vec<SectionLocation>, location: SectionLocation) {
    if !locations.contains(&location) {
        locations.push(location);
    }
}
---

@s Indexing One Code Occurrence

A code occurrence first resolves to the stable identity it affects. For the
first addition to `Greeting line`, that is `blocks[1]`; its visible layout
location is section 3. The `+=` modifier selects `additions`, while `:=`
selects `redefinitions` and an unmodified base selects `definition`.
`noWeave` records a hidden base but contributes no visible location.

--- Index a code occurrence
let Some(code) = block.code() else {
    continue;
};
let BlockLookup::Found(identity) =
    resolved.lookup(chapter_index, &code.name)
else {
    continue;
};
let locations = blocks.entry(identity).or_default();
let hidden = code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::NoWeave);
if code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::Additive) {
    if let Some(location) =
        layout.block_location(chapter_index, section_index, block_index)
    {
        push_unique(&mut locations.additions, location.clone());
    }
} else if code.modifiers.contains(&Modifier::Redefinition) {
    if let Some(location) =
        layout.block_location(chapter_index, section_index, block_index)
    {
        push_unique(&mut locations.redefinitions, location.clone());
    }
} else if hidden {
    locations.definition_hidden = true;
} else if let Some(location) =
    layout.block_location(chapter_index, section_index, block_index)
{
    locations.definition = Some(location.clone());
}
---

@s Indexing Whole-Line Uses

The line inside the `hello.txt` root is a whole-line reference to
`Greeting line`. Lookup finds `blocks[1]`, and the root occurrence's layout
location records section 1 in that identity's `uses` list.

Only a visible code occurrence can supply a visible use location. Undefined or
ambiguous references have no target identity here; rendering diagnoses them
when it reaches the visible line.

--- Index whole-line uses
let Some(location) =
    layout.block_location(chapter_index, section_index, block_index)
else {
    continue;
};
for line in &block.lines {
    if let Some(name) = block_reference(&line.text)
        && let BlockLookup::Found(target) =
            resolved.lookup(chapter_index, name)
    {
        push_unique(
            &mut blocks.entry(target).or_default().uses,
            location.clone(),
        );
    }
}
---

@s One Meaning, Two Syntaxes

Preparation has made all format-independent decisions. `Distance` showed one
section whose equation, code reference, and definition have one shared
meaning. `Greeting` extended the same model to five presentation sections,
forward links, additions, a replacement, and a use. Identifier locations pass
through the same occurrence-to-section lookup.

The following chapters may now choose HTML pages or one LaTeX document without
changing any of those answers. Their remaining responsibility is to express
the prepared facts safely and readably in their own output syntax.
